NetPi private-library installation -- updated 2026-10-11
======================================================

README.md describes this unofficial LAME 4.0 maintenance fork, its source
provenance, changes and license. CONTRIBUTING.md describes development and
regression testing. This document covers the optional Linux installer.

Requirements and platform scope
-------------------------------

scripts/build-netpi.py targets GNU/Linux with GCC, GNU make/binutils/coreutils,
Python >= 3.11 and a dynamically linked FFmpeg built with libmp3lame support.
It uses the Linux dynamic loader's LD_PRELOAD and loaded-object tracing.
Build natively on the target: installation checks execute the newly built
library, and PGO training executes it through FFmpeg. Other platform builds use
the ordinary upstream build machinery documented in README.md.

For Debian or Ubuntu, the base dependencies can be installed with:

  sudo apt-get install build-essential python3 ffmpeg

Build and install from the source root:

  python3 scripts/build-netpi.py
  python3 scripts/build-netpi.py --check
  python3 scripts/build-netpi.py --force

The default prefix is /opt/netpi/lame-4.0. Compilation runs as the invoking user;
sudo is used for installation when the prefix is not writable. --prefix accepts
an absolute writable location outside the source tree. Paths containing spaces
or colons are rejected because the dynamic loader uses them as separators.

Build identity and upgrades
---------------------------

The active lib/libmp3lame.so.0 is a symlink to builds/<input-id>-<binary-sha256>/.
Each build retains manifest.json, config.log and configure/build output. Build
identity includes source content, compiler, flags, profile, relevant toolchain
settings and training-corpus hashes. Matching verified builds are reused;
--force or --rebuild requests recompilation.

The new library is checked before the active symlink is switched. Existing
builds remain available for conversions that already resolved their library
path. Keep files inside builds/ immutable. The installer uses a private prefix
and leaves system package-managed libraries in place.

Optional profiles
-----------------

Evaluate one change at a time on the actual device:

  python3 scripts/build-netpi.py --profile cortex-a53
  python3 scripts/build-netpi.py --lto
  python3 scripts/build-netpi.py --neon
  python3 scripts/build-netpi.py --fast-log yes
  python3 scripts/build-netpi.py --fp-model strict
  python3 scripts/build-netpi.py --pgo-corpus /path/to/representative-lossless-music

The cortex-a53 profile requires an AArch64 compiler and native execution. It
controls CPU selection: direct -march, -mtune and -mcpu options in CFLAGS,
CPPFLAGS, LDFLAGS or LIBS are rejected. Remove those options or select the generic
profile to retain custom CPU flags. NEON is off by default; enabling it on an
unsupported architecture fails explicitly.

The generic profile starts with -O3 and -ffast-math. Explicit floating-point
options are applied after CFLAGS; --fp-model strict selects -fno-fast-math.
Fast-log's auto mode preserves upstream architecture defaults. --ieee-hack
selects the historical quantization path for comparisons. These settings can
change encoded bytes and need separate quality and performance evaluation.

LTO and PGO target GCC. PGO trains both mono and stereo V0 on WAV/FLAC files and
then rebuilds with the collected profiles. Use a representative corpus that
you are entitled to use. Training audio is not uploaded or installed; the
manifest records relative names and content hashes. Compiler/build logs are
retained when a build fails.

Validation and integration
--------------------------

The automated regression and installer checks are described in CONTRIBUTING.md
and .github/workflows/ci.yml. They cover native Linux execution, sanitizers,
AArch64 under QEMU, output decoding and scalar/NEON equality.

Native Raspberry Pi speed, peak memory, temperature and playback interference
still need device measurements with the same input corpus and workload.
Level-matched ABX listening is a separate check for perceived quality. No
measured ARM speedup or audible improvement is claimed by this fork.

NetPi uses FFmpeg planar PCM, V0 and separate rsgain analysis. Its application
integration selects the private library for encoding subprocesses. The source
build and tests in this repository run independently of that application.
For batch throughput, compare worker counts of 1 and 2 under real playback and
thermal load before choosing a setting.

Legacy quantization-table initialization is shared within a process. Concurrent
instance setup is not guaranteed to be thread-safe; NetPi's separate FFmpeg
processes do not share those tables. See HACKING for initialization ownership.
